WASHINGTON, D.C.  Congressman John Sarbanes (D-Md.) today voted for the Violence Against Women Reauthorization Act (H.R. 1585), a bipartisan bill to help reduce domestic violence and sexual assault against women, and extend critical support and resources for victims and survivors in Maryland and across the country. The bill passed the U.S. House of Representatives by a vote of 263 to 158.
It's our responsibility as a nation not only to protect all Americans from domestic violence and sexual assault, but also to provide victims and survivors with the care, resources and services that they need, said Congressman Sarbanes. Today, we took an important step forward in that effort. Moving forward, I will continue working tirelessly with my colleagues to help root out the scourge of domestic violence and sexual assault in America.
Among its many provisions, the Violence Against Women Reauthorization Act:
 Makes vital new investments in domestic violence prevention;

	Expands and improves services for victims of domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault and stalking;

	Gives law enforcement enhanced tools to combat domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault and stalking and to improve community safety;

	Strengthens the health care system's response to domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault and stalking;

	Helps better protect Native American women by reaffirming tribal criminal jurisdiction over non-Indian perpetrators of domestic violence, dating violence, sexual assault and stalking;

	Preserves and improves housing protections for survivors and supports survivors who need assistance rebuilding financially;

	Protects the Office on Violence Against Women; and

	Closes loopholes to help prevent intimate partner homicides by prohibiting persons convicted of misdemeanor stalking or dating violence from possessing firearms.

 A coalition of more than 1,300 organizations  known as the National Task force to End Sexual and Domestic Violence Against Women  supports H.R. 1585. This coalition includes the National Coalition Against Domestic Violence, National Network to End Domestic Violence, National Coalition of Anti-Violence Programs, Break the Cycle, Legal Momentum, The Leadership Conference on Civil and Human Rights, National Organization for Women, MomsRising, Feminist Majority, YWCA USA, AAUW, Business and Professional Women's Foundation, National Partnership for Women and Families, National Women's Law Center, National Association of Hispanic Organizations, AFL-CIO, UAW, NAACP, Human Rights Campaign, National Council of Churches and National Congress of American Indians.
